; Regression: a method call's argument TYPES were never compared when the
; concept declared exactly ONE method of that name (2026-08-23).
;
; lookup_method# owns method_params_match#, the single place a declared
; parameter type ever meets an argument type — and it ran it only inside
; `if candidates.length > 1`, the s163 overload SELECTION loop. With one
; candidate there is nothing to select, so the function was taken by name and
; nothing looked at what was being handed to it.
;
; Nothing downstream catches it either: emit_call synthesizes the callee's type
; from the ARGUMENT VALUES rather than from the declaration, so llc is handed a
; self-consistent call and has nothing to object to. The callee then reads the
; wrong thing out of the register — here a String struct where an int is
; declared.
;
; The same mistake on a FREE function has always been a hard `function not
; found`, because check_function_match# compares unconditionally. That
; asymmetry between the two call shapes is what this gate closes.
;
; It found two live sites in packages/dazzle the day it landed, both of the
; shape that survives on layout coincidence: Interpreter.emit_aux declared
; MessageType while all twelve callers passed a MessageFragment (MessageType's
; field 0 IS a MessageFragment), and Primitive.invalid_key_arg passed a
; ref[Identifier] where StringC was declared (Identifier's field 0 IS its
; name). Both read the right bytes, and neither declaration was true.
;
; xfail: argument type mismatch for Box.take: argument 1 is String, expected int

define Box
(
    v: int
)
{
    function take(this, n: int) returns int
    {
        n * 2
    }
}

let b Box(7)
let r b.take("nope")
print "unreachable"
